# Cursor seats — identity and boundaries

Default posture is **readiness bridge**: an ordinary Cursor chat orients and
reports but never claims a seat, mutates the mailbox, consumes a cursor, claims
a lock, or pushes. A live Pipeline seat exists only when the Cursor seat
launcher binds it from a committed trigger. Full adapter:
`docs/protocol/cursor/continuation.md`.

## When a seat is bound

- `director`/`director2` implement (behavior source `director`);
  `operator`/`operator2` verify (behavior source `operator2`); `coordinator`
  reconciles and never authors production work or holds a cursor.
- Behavior-changing acceptance needs a non-author Operator GO from a distinct
  seat using a different system-visible model, emitted through the fixed mailbox
  writer. An Operator cannot verify work it authored.
- Ordinary Git and pytest use `env -u GIT_INDEX_FILE`; the per-seat index
  `.git/index-cursor-<seat>` is only for deliberate staging. Read-only Git
  stays usable bare under a seat index; index mutators and pytest need the
  prefix.
- Provider separation: never launch another provider's seats
  (`codex-seat`, `agy-seat`, Claude sessions) from Cursor; reading their
  files is fine.

## Separately authorized effects

Edit, stage, commit, push, merge, mailbox publish, cursor consume, lock, and
paid spend are distinct authorities. Publish/consume go through
`coordination/bin/cursor-publish` / `cursor-consume`, which only delegate to the
existing fixed writers. Never write `coordination/mailbox/` or `.cursor/runtime/`
directly. The project hooks fail closed on these; the hook describes identity
and grants no authority.

## Cursor subagents are advisors

An unbound Cursor session may use Task subagents as parent-scoped advisors.
Cursor's child-tool hook inputs cannot safely distinguish a live-seat child from
its parent, so a live seat cannot spawn a subagent. Use a separate unbound
advisor session instead. Advisor output is evidence for its parent, not a
mailbox event, cursor advance, operator verdict, coordinator route, lock action,
push, or spend authorization.
